✅ Approach 2: Creating a Thread by Implementing the Runnable Interface

In this approach:

🔴 Runnable is a functional interface present in the java.lang package. It represents a task that can be executed by a thread.
🔴 Create a class that implements the Runnable interface.
🔴 Override the run() method to define the task to be executed.
🔴 Create an object of the Runnable class. At this point, only a Runnable object is created. No thread exists.
🔴 Create a Thread object and pass the Runnable object to its constructor. At this point, only a Thread object is created (state = NEW).
🔴 Call start(). The JVM creates a new thread, schedules it for execution, and eventually the Thread object invokes the Runnable object's run() method.

Flow:

Main Thread
    |
    |
creates Runnable object
    |
    ↓
Creates Thread object with Runnable task
    |
    |
calls start()
    |
    ↓
JVM creates new Thread
    |
    ↓
New thread executes run()